    "slug": "net-revenue",
    "title": "Net Revenue: Discounts, Returns and Gross-to-Net Reconciliation",
    "seoTitle": "Net Revenue: Discounts, Returns and Cash",
    "description": "Reconcile gross sales, discounts, returns and allowances to net revenue. Follow the same example into receivables, processor fees and bank cash.",
    "metaDescription": "Reconcile gross sales, discounts, returns and allowances to net revenue. Follow the same example into receivables, processor fees and bank cash.",
    "primaryKeyword": "net revenue",
    "scopeBoundary": "Owns gross-to-net sales deductions and the revenue-to-collection boundary, not gross profit after COGS, business net income or employee gross-to-net pay.",
    "opening": "Net revenue is the revenue amount remaining after the applicable sales deductions on a stated reporting basis. Reconcile earned gross sales, discounts, returns and allowances without subtracting unrelated operating costs. Then follow receivables, collections and fees separately: net revenue, gross profit, net income and bank deposits are different measures.",
    "answerHtml": "<p>Net revenue is the revenue amount remaining after the applicable sales deductions on a stated reporting basis. Reconcile earned gross sales, discounts, returns and allowances without subtracting unrelated operating costs. Then follow receivables, collections and fees separately: net revenue, gross profit, net income and bank deposits are different measures.</p>",
